  "response": "A court confronted with duelling reconstructions of an accident does not begin by preferring one expert to the other; it begins by identifying the legal test that the evidence must satisfy and the party who must satisfy it. A plaintiff who must prove causation on the balance of probabilities cannot at the same time be afforded the material contribution test, which applies precisely where but-for causation cannot be established scientifically; the plaintiff does bear that balance-of-probabilities burden, and that test does apply in cases of scientific impossibility, but the one cannot be squared with the other. The but-for test, by contrast with the material contribution standard, asks whether the injury would have occurred in the absence of the defendant's conduct, a question that invites a counterfactual rather than a probabilistic judgment. Once a breach of duty is established, the burden of proving causation shifts to the defendant; that reallocation rules out any continuing requirement that the plaintiff establish causation to the balance of probabilities, and the two allocations cannot both govern a single trial.\n\nThe question of an intervening act proceeds along a second axis, concerned not with the sufficiency of proof but with the limits of attribution. Novus actus interveniens describes an intervening act that severs the causal connection between a breach and the harm. A reasonably foreseeable intervening act does not break the chain of causation. A criminal act by a third party automatically relieves the defendant of all liability for the plaintiff's injury, and that cannot be squared with the principle that an intervening act falling within the range of reasonable foresight leaves the causal chain unbroken. On the evidence accepted in this case, the third party's act broke the chain of causation; that is to say, the intervening act of the third party severed the legal link between the defendant's breach and the plaintiff's injury.\n\nBoth inquiries depend upon opinion evidence that has survived judicial scrutiny. Federal Rule of Evidence 702 governs the admissibility of expert opinion testimony, and it is from that provision that the authority to keep out an opinion resting on unreliable method flows. It is because Daubert v. Merrell Dow Pharmaceuticals established the trial judge's gatekeeping role over expert evidence that a trial judge may exclude expert testimony resting on an unreliable methodology. The resolution of conflicting expert testimony is a question for the trier of fact.\n\nBecause the choice between competing expert reconstructions of the sequence of events falls to the fact-finder, the trial court ultimately held that the defendant's conduct remained a proximate cause of the plaintiff's injury. That holding and the severance of the legal link between the breach and the plaintiff's injury by the third party's act cannot both be true; a finding of continuing proximate cause rules out any such severance. The choice between incompatible narratives is thus made within the ordinary machinery of proof rather than by any special rule reserved for conflicts of expertise.",
